I'm trying to track down the cause(s) of some SCSI errors that I'm
getting. 

Almost always the errors occur at night during backups. The errors
either show up just as syslog entries or as a hung machine (no ping, no
login, but I can SysRq).

I've determined that the errors occur while creating nightly backups.
These backups are bzip2-compressed tar files (a few MB to 7 GB), that
read from the root partition (/dev/sda4) and write to an IDE partition
(/dev/hda1). They have never occurred (except once) during the day.

Are these errors typical of a hardware problem or of a software problem?

So far, I've taken the following steps:

0. Read the "Linux 2.4 SCSI subsystem HOWTO" (dated 2002-04-07)

1. Enabled SCSI logging with

      echo "scsi log error 5"   > /proc/scsi/scsi
      echo "scsi log timeout 1" > /proc/scsi/scsi

   Are there more useful parameters to use, keeping in mind that my
   /var/log/messages file _is_ on the SCSI disk that's causing problems?

2. Monitoring hourly the number of bad blocks on /dev/sda with

     scu -f /dev/sda show defects

   Currently, the "Defect list length is 5824 bytes, containing 728
   defects" (Is this a lot, little, normal for an 18GB Quantum Atlas 10K
   drive?)

Is there anything else I should do to track down these (example log
entries below) problems?

Thanks.

